// the message is folded into a constant with the source location, so it has to be readable at
// compile time. the parameter's *type* is satisfied here - `$m` is a `string`, which is what the
// overload takes - so this is the case that shows the requirement is about literal-ness and not
// about types, and that it does not lift now that a `string` type exists
string $m = "not a literal at the call site";
die($m);
